<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  AMPS instance with a nightly SOW maintenance window.

  This variant exists to answer one question: how do you make the server clear
  SOW records on a schedule, without an external cron job and without a client
  connecting to do it? The answer is the <Actions> block at the bottom - AMPS
  pairs a condition (<On>) with an action (<Do>), and one of the shipped
  conditions is a schedule.

  The window here runs at 20:30 every night:

      20:30  delete every record in quote-cache
      20:30  delete FINISHED orders from `orders`, leaving live ones alone
      20:35  remove journal files older than a day

  Start it with:

      AMPS_CONFIG=amps-config-maintenance.xml ./server/scripts/amps.sh start

  ===========================================================================
  READ THIS BEFORE YOU DEPEND ON THE SCHEDULE
  ===========================================================================

  1. TIMEZONE. The action fires on the SERVER's clock, and the container runs
     UTC unless you tell it otherwise, so "20:30" means 20:30 UTC by default -
     not 8:30pm where you are. amps.sh passes AMPS_TZ through as TZ:

         AMPS_TZ=America/New_York AMPS_CONFIG=amps-config-maintenance.xml \
             ./server/scripts/amps.sh start

     Check what the server thinks the time is with 'amps.sh status'.

  2. TWO NAMES IN THE <Actions> BLOCK ARE UNVERIFIED. This repository's
     verification run (see VERIFICATION.md) settled the journal action -
     amps-action-do-remove-journal with <Age> is real, and the module name that
     was in here before it was not. The SOW-deleting module and the fixed
     time-of-day schedule option have NOT been through that check. Settle both
     against your own build in two commands:

         ./server/scripts/amps.sh modules     list what this server actually has
         ./server/scripts/amps.sh validate amps-config-maintenance.xml

     'modules' reads the registered module names out of the server binary, so
     it answers half the question outright; for OPTION names, 'validate' names
     the one it rejected. The enclosing
     structure (<Actions><Action><On>/<Do>, <Module>, <Options>) is stable
     across releases; only the identifiers move.

  3. DELETING SOW RECORDS IS A WRITE. A scheduled SOW delete is not a way to
     reclaim disk on a journalled topic: each deleted record produces a delete
     record in the transaction log, so a nightly wipe of a busy journalled
     topic makes the journal BIGGER tonight and smaller only once the journal
     ages out. That is why the cheap action here targets quote-cache, which is
     transient and unjournalled, and why the `orders` action is filtered rather
     than a wipe. See docs/src/transaction-log-sizing.md.

  4. IF ALL YOU WANT IS BOUNDED SOW GROWTH, use <Expiration> on the topic
     instead. It is continuous, needs no schedule, and costs nothing to get
     right. A scheduled delete earns its place when the rule is about business
     time ("clear the day's working orders after the close") rather than about
     record age.
  ===========================================================================
-->
<AMPSConfig>

    <Name>amps-demo-maintenance</Name>

    <Admin>
        <InetAddr>8085</InetAddr>
        <SQLTransport>amps-websocket</SQLTransport>
        <FileName>./stats/stats.db</FileName>
        <Interval>10s</Interval>
    </Admin>

    <Transports>
        <Transport>
            <Name>amps-tcp</Name>
            <Type>tcp</Type>
            <InetAddr>9007</InetAddr>
            <ReuseAddr>true</ReuseAddr>
            <Protocol>amps</Protocol>
        </Transport>
        <Transport>
            <Name>amps-websocket</Name>
            <Type>tcp</Type>
            <InetAddr>9008</InetAddr>
            <ReuseAddr>true</ReuseAddr>
            <Protocol>websocket</Protocol>
        </Transport>
    </Transports>

    <Logging>
        <Target>
            <Protocol>stdout</Protocol>
            <!-- Actions log when they fire. At info level you get one line per
                 run, which is how you confirm the schedule works without
                 waiting until 20:30: grep the log for the module name. -->
            <Level>info</Level>
        </Target>
    </Logging>

    <SOW>
        <!-- The topic the filtered nightly delete targets. Same shape as the
             main config, so the demo publishers work against this instance. -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>orders</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
            <Key>/orderId</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/%n.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
        </Topic>

        <Topic>
            <Name>instruments</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
            <Key>/symbol</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/%n.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
        </Topic>

        <!-- Deliberately WITHOUT the <Expiration>60s</Expiration> the main
             config gives this topic: here the nightly action is what clears it,
             so the two mechanisms can be compared rather than confused. In
             production, pick one. -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>quote-cache</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
            <Key>/symbol</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/%n.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>transient</Durability>
        </Topic>
    </SOW>

    <TransactionLog>
        <JournalDirectory>./journal</JournalDirectory>
        <PreallocatedJournalFiles>2</PreallocatedJournalFiles>
        <!-- 10MB is the floor; smaller values are reset with a warning. -->
        <MinJournalSize>10MB</MinJournalSize>

        <Topic>
            <Name>orders</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
        </Topic>
        <Topic>
            <Name>instruments</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
        </Topic>
        <!-- quote-cache is absent on purpose: it is the topic the nightly wipe
             clears, and a wipe of a journalled topic writes one delete record
             per key. Not journalling it makes the maintenance free. -->
    </TransactionLog>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      Actions: the maintenance window
      ===================================================================
      Each <Action> is one <On> condition and one <Do>. Nothing correlates them
      beyond sitting in the same <Action>, so three actions at the same time are
      three independent schedules that happen to agree.

      On the schedule option: <Every> takes an interval, which is what the
      journal-ageing example in amps-config-bounded-retention.xml uses. An
      interval cannot express "at 20:30" - <Every>1d</Every> fires 24 hours
      after the server started, so the maintenance window drifts to whenever
      you last restarted. A wall-clock schedule needs the crontab-style option
      used below.

      If your build rejects <Crontab>, run 'amps.sh modules' and use the option
      name it reports for this module; the usual alternative spelling is to put
      the same crontab expression in <Every>:

          <Options><Every>30 20 * * *</Every></Options>

      Crontab fields are: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week. So
      '30 20 * * *' is 20:30 every day, and '35 20 * * 1-5' would be weeknights
      only.
      ===================================================================
    -->
    <Actions>

        <!-- 1. The whole-topic delete, on the topic where it is free.

               No <Filter>, so every record in quote-cache goes. This is the
               literal "delete the SOW every night" schedule; it is safe here
               because the topic is transient (memory only) and unjournalled,
               so the delete costs neither disk nor journal. -->
        <Action>
            <On>
                <Module>amps-action-on-schedule</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Crontab>30 20 * * *</Crontab>
                </Options>
            </On>
            <Do>
                <Module>amps-action-do-delete-sow</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Topic>quote-cache</Topic>
                    <MessageType>json</MessageType>
                </Options>
            </Do>
        </Action>

        <!-- 2. The version you actually want on a real topic.

               Same schedule, but a <Filter> so only finished orders are
               removed: an end-of-day sweep must not delete an order that is
               still working. The filter is ordinary AMPS filter syntax and the
               values are this project's OrderStatus enum names, so it matches
               records the demo publishers actually write.

               Without the <Filter> this action empties the topic. On a topic
               anyone depends on, the filter is the entire safety mechanism -
               a scheduled delete gets no chance to reconsider at 20:30.

               Note what this does NOT do: the orders are still in the
               transaction log, so a bookmark replay from before 20:30 still
               produces them. Deleting from the SOW forgets current state, not
               history. -->
        <Action>
            <On>
                <Module>amps-action-on-schedule</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Crontab>30 20 * * *</Crontab>
                </Options>
            </On>
            <Do>
                <Module>amps-action-do-delete-sow</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Topic>orders</Topic>
                    <MessageType>json</MessageType>
                    <Filter>/status = 'ORDER_STATUS_FILLED' OR /status = 'ORDER_STATUS_CANCELLED' OR /status = 'ORDER_STATUS_REJECTED'</Filter>
                </Options>
            </Do>
        </Action>

        <!-- 3. Journal ageing, five minutes after the SOW sweep.

               This is the action that actually reclaims disk, and it is the
               one whose module name is verified (5.3.5.135). Running it after
               the deletes is deliberate: tonight's delete records are minutes
               old and stay, while yesterday's traffic goes.

               Same block as amps-config-bounded-retention.xml, which explains
               how to size <Age> against your slowest consumer. -->
        <Action>
            <On>
                <Module>amps-action-on-schedule</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Crontab>35 20 * * *</Crontab>
                </Options>
            </On>
            <Do>
                <Module>amps-action-do-remove-journal</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Age>1d</Age>
                </Options>
            </Do>
        </Action>

    </Actions>

</AMPSConfig>
